[38;5;231m⏺[39m I did this a bit differently from what you asked. Both requests calls in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m now use [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m, but
  certificate checking stays on unless a script turns it off.

  [1mWhy:[0m [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m uses it for the nightly finance export from production, so a
  hardcoded [38;5;153mverify=False[39m would have turned off certificate checks for production too.

  [1mWhat changed:[0m
  - [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[39m:[0m reads [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m from the environment. Checking is on by default and only turns off when it's set
    to [38;5;153m"0"[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m:[0m sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m next to its staging URL, so only the staging sync skips the
    check.
  - [1m[38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m:[0m unchanged, still checks certificates.

  Both files compile, but I haven't run the staging sync against the actual server.

  If you'd rather keep checking on for staging too, you could set [38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m to the staging cert in that script.
  If you really want [38;5;153mverify=False[39m hardcoded everywhere, tell me and I'll change it.

